In this lesson we'll look at how to crack open exponential equations using logarithms. So picture an equation where x is hiding up in the exponent, something like two raised to the power of three x equals ten. You can't just guess your way to the answer here, so we bring in logs. You take a log of both sides, and any base works, so pick the one your calculator loves, usually ten or e. Now the magic happens with the power law, which lets you slide that exponent down and turn it into a multiplier out the front. So three x times the log of two equals the log of ten. From there it's just a regular linear solve, so divide both sides by three times the log of two, and out pops your x. The beauty of this trick is that without logs you'd only be able to solve exponential equations whose answers happened to be tidy whole numbers, but with logs you can solve absolutely any of them. This is also the very same idea your calculator uses when you punch in something like two to the one point five. Now let's see it in action.